---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
title: "Getting Started";
description: "A quick introduction to using Umi‑Time — a planetary way to see the UTC day.";
---

<Base title="Getting Started with Umi‑Time">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <section class="page">
      <h1>Getting Started</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Umi‑Time gives you a single, planetary time of day: one shared value
        everywhere on Earth, interpreted locally through longitude and solar
        context. This page helps you see it, understand it, and use it within a
        minute.
      </p>

      <!-- 1. What You Can Do Here -->
      <section>
        <h2>What You Can Do Here</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>View Umi‑Time in your browser</li>
          <li>Understand how it relates to civil time and UTC</li>
          <li>Use the interactive clock</li>
          <li>Install the library for your own projects</li>
          <li>Convert between Umi‑Time, UTC and local civil time</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. Clock Demo -->
      <section>
        <h2>See Umi‑Time in Action</h2>
        <p>
          The Umi‑Time analog clock is the reference analog implementation of
          Umi‑Time and often the simplest way to understand the system.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a class="button" href="/clock">Open the Clock Demo</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Understanding the Basics -->
      <section>
        <h2>Understanding the Basics</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time expresses the day as a smooth, uninterrupted 360‑unit cycle
          derived directly from UTC. Every place on Earth shares the same umi
          value at the same moment, with local meaning derived from
          longitude-based interpretation.
        </p>

        <p>Umi‑Time builds on UTC through a layered model</p>
        <ol>
          <li>UTC provides the universal time reference.</li>
          <li>Umi‑Time expresses the UTC day as a 360‑umi cycle.</li>
          <li>
            Longitude-based local interpretation shows where that universal
            value falls within the observer's day without using time zones.
          </li>
          <li>
            Optional solar context layers provide sunrise, sunset, twilight, and
            other astronomical context related to the observer's location.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          These layers never alter the underlying umi value. Everyone on Earth
          shares the same umi value at the same moment. The layers only provide
          progressively richer ways to interpret that value locally.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Using Umi‑Time Day to Day -->
      <section>
        <h2>Using Umi‑Time Day to Day</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time helps you understand where the world is in the day at a
          glance. Because everyone shares the same umi value, global
          coordination becomes simpler, while local interpretation preserves an
          intuitive sense of morning, afternoon, evening, and night.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Developer Quickstart -->
      <section>
        <h2>Developer Quickstart</h2>
        <p>
          If you want to use Umi‑Time programmatically, here is a simple
          example:
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`// Get the current Umi‑Time value
import { UmiTime } from "umi-time";
import { UMI_EXTENDED } from "umi-time";

const now = new Date();
const umi = new UmiTime(now);

const text = umi.format(UMI_EXTENDED);
console.log(text);
`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />

        <p>
          See the <a href="/api">Developer API Reference</a> for full details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Next Steps -->
      <section>
        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/faq">FAQ</a></li>
          <li><a href="/design">Design Principles</a></li>
          <li><a href="/api">Developer API Reference</a></li>
          <li><a href="/examples">Examples & Demos</a></li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/umi-time" target="_blank">GitHub</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
